## Kernel Design

The kernel uses static memory only. There is no heap allocation.

The task descriptor stores:

    task id
    parent task id
    priority
    run state
    saved stack pointer
    entry function pointer
    ready queue next pointer
    send queue next pointer
    per-task send queue head and tail
    send message pointer and length
    reply buffer pointer and length
    receive buffer pointer and length
    receive sender-tid pointer

The current task states are:

    TASK_UNUSED
    TASK_READY
    TASK_ACTIVE
    TASK_EXITED
    TASK_SEND_BLOCKED
    TASK_RECEIVE_BLOCKED
    TASK_REPLY_BLOCKED

Each task has a statically allocated stack. Each task stack stores a saved context frame used during context switching.

## Scheduling

The scheduler uses static priorities.

Lower numeric priority values represent higher scheduling priority. Each priority has a FIFO ready queue. Tasks at the same priority are scheduled round-robin.

## Message Passing Design

Send / Receive / Reply are implemented in the kernel.

If a task calls Send to a receiver that is already receive-blocked, the kernel copies the message immediately into the receiver's buffer, records the sender tid, moves the receiver to ready state, and places the sender into reply-blocked state.

If the receiver has not called Receive yet, the sender is placed into the receiver's send queue and becomes send-blocked.

If a task calls Receive and a sender is already waiting in its send queue, the kernel dequeues the sender, copies the message into the receiver's buffer, records the sender tid, and makes the sender reply-blocked.

If a task calls Receive and there are no waiting senders, the receiver becomes receive-blocked.

Reply copies the reply message into the original sender's reply buffer and makes the sender ready again.

